course: dictionary
lesson: 12099
title: Coeval: the same age or time
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Coeval is a formal word. It means of the same age, or existing at the same time as something else. When you read that two things are coeval, you learn that they belong to the same period. «The old well and the stone wall were coeval, both built in the same century.»
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «My grandad and his best friend are coeval; they were both born in 1950.» The words born in 1950 tell you they are the same age, so coeval must mean the same age.
explain: Coeval can also be a noun, meaning a person or thing of the same age. «At the party, Maya talked to her coevals, the children in her class who were also ten.» Coevals means children of the same age.
explain: Everyday English says from the same time or the same age. Swap one of those phrases into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have found the meaning.
fact: Coeval means of the same age, or from the same time.
fact: Coeval is a formal word, often found in history, archaeology and science writing.
fact: Coeval can be an adjective («coeval buildings») or a noun («her coevals», meaning people of her age).
fact: «The cave paintings are coeval with the first farms» means the paintings and the farms come from the same time.
fact: Check a new word by swapping in a simpler one, such as from the same time. If the sentence still makes sense, you are right.
exercise: «The two castles were coeval, both built in the 1200s.» What does coeval mean? | Very tall | *Of the same age | Made of wood | Lost forever
exercise: «Ella and her coevals all started school in the same year.» Coevals here means? | Animals on a farm | Strangers from far away | *People of the same age as Ella | Teachers at the school
exercise: «The fossil and the old volcanic rock were coeval.» Find and copy the word that means the same age or time. | volcanic | fossil | rock | *coeval
test: «The painted pots and the first farm tools were coeval.» What does coeval suggest? | They are shiny and new | They are very heavy | They are next to each other on a shelf | *They come from the same time
test: Which phrase could replace coeval without changing the meaning in «My sister and I are coeval»? | Much taller | Very old | Far apart | *The same age
exam: Which is the best meaning of coeval? | Far away in space | Very old and broken | *Of the same age or existing at the same time | Brand new and shiny
